(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-vignettes"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient treated for melanoma asks what she should be doing between appointments. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including scalp, back, palms, soles and nails","Monthly self-examination of sun-exposed skin only, since melanoma does not arise on the scalp, back, palms, soles or nails","Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, since more frequent checking causes anxiety","No self-examination of any site, since skin surveillance is entirely the specialist team's responsibility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the sun-protected and acral sites are the ones people skip. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has an actinic keratosis on her forearm. Which single distinction does the deck call the most important when assessing it?","choices":["Verruca, when the lesion is warty and interrupts the skin lines","Early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ","Seborrheic keratosis, when the lesion is waxy and stuck-on","Solar lentigo, when the lesion is flat and uniformly pigmented"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — thickness, induration, ulceration or bleeding all raise it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"One of that 71-year-old's scalp lesions is now thickened, indurated and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Treat with liquid nitrogen cryotherapy and review in three months, since these are expected features","Begin field-directed fluorouracil to the whole scalp and review at the end of the course","Reassure and review in a year, since actinic keratoses commonly involute without treatment","Order a shave or punch biopsy, because induration and bleeding raise concern for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those features are not part of a typical actinic keratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a melanoma in situ confirmed on his forearm. Which re-excision margin does the deck give?","choices":["0.5 to 1 cm","1 to 2 cm","A uniform 1 cm regardless of thickness","3 to 5 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — margins widen with Breslow thickness from there. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient treated for squamous cell carcinoma of the scalp returns with new numbness and tingling in the distribution of the scar. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Repeat topical fluorouracil to the scar and review in three months","Arrange routine review in twelve months as part of annual surveillance","Refer urgently, because neurologic symptoms are one of the deck's urgent referral triggers","Reassure, because sensory change is expected permanently after any scalp excision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they raise concern for perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Four patients each have a pigmented band in a nail. Which pattern of longitudinal melanonychia should worry you most?","choices":["A new or evolving band in ONE digit, increasing in width","Uniform narrow bands in SEVERAL nails, present since childhood and unchanged in width or colour","A band that grows out distally over several months after a crush injury","A band present in all twenty nails since adolescence, identical in every digit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — single-digit change with widening is the concerning pattern. The full description also includes irregular colour, thickness or spacing of the lines, and proximal widening or a triangular shape. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 39-year-old has a 4 mm pigmented lesion that is asymmetric with an irregular border and has darkened over three months. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Biopsy or excise it, because smaller lesions can still be melanoma","Reassure and review in a year, because a lesion under 6 mm cannot be melanoma","Photograph and review in six months, because evolution over three months is too rapid for melanoma","Refer for sentinel lymph node biopsy before sampling the lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the 6 mm figure is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient has just had a basal cell carcinoma excised and asks whether that is the end of it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Recurrence at the same site is near-certain, so a further excision should be booked now","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to 50% of patients, so at least annual full-skin examination is mandatory","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in under 1% of patients, so no further surveillance is needed","A second basal cell carcinoma develops in up to 50% of patients, so examination every five years is sufficient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives lifelong surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 61-year-old kidney transplant recipient develops Kaposi sarcoma. Which is the most appropriate management step, and what caution applies?","choices":["Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel first, since iatrogenic disease does not respond to dose reduction","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses where clinically feasible, coordinating with the transplant team before making any change","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses immediately without involving the transplant team, since the malignancy takes priority","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, since immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the transplant team must be involved before doses change. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 41-year-old describes severe stabbing pain under one fingernail, worse in cold water, with an exquisitely tender point. The nail itself looks almost normal, with a small red-blue spot beneath it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Glomus tumour","Nail unit melanoma","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma","Subungual haematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the triad of paroxysmal pain, point tenderness and cold sensitivity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a pigmented lesion excised, reported as melanoma with a positive sentinel node. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Arrange routine dermatology follow-up, since a positive sentinel node does not alter the management pathway or the referral threshold","Repeat the sentinel node biopsy to confirm the finding before any referral to an expert centre is made","Begin topical imiquimod to the excision site while awaiting further staging and imaging","Refer to an expert centre, since nodal spread is an explicit referral trigger and coordinated dermatology and surgical oncology care is warranted"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — nodal or other-site spread crosses the threshold regardless of thickness. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man newly diagnosed with human immunodeficiency virus has several red-purple plaques on his legs. Which examination does the deck call essential, and what might you find?","choices":["Scalp examination — follicular lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Oral examination — hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Nail examination — subungual lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Ocular examination — eyelid lesions are common and may be the presenting site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the mouth may declare the disease before anywhere else. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 58-year-old patient asks you to look at one abnormal nail. Which preparatory step does the deck specify before inspection?","choices":["Leave the polish in place to avoid disturbing the nail plate, and inspect the periungual skin only","Inspect the affected nail under a Wood lamp before removing any polish","Remove the polish and inspect every nail, the periungual skin, the palms and soles, and the regional nodes","Remove the polish and inspect the affected nail alone, since comparison with other nails is unhelpful"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a single-nail problem is assessed against all the others. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A patient with cutaneous T-cell lymphoma develops enlarged inguinal nodes. What does the deck say these may represent, and what is required?","choices":["They indicate regional infection rather than lymphoma and warrant a course of oral antibiotics","They may be benign dermatopathic change or lymphoma involvement, and require directed biopsy or imaging to distinguish","They always indicate systemic lymphoma involvement and warrant immediate systemic chemotherapy without any directed biopsy or imaging","They are always benign dermatopathic change and need no directed biopsy, imaging or further assessment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — enlargement alone does not establish spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has a pigmented lesion on the nose with stippled pigment but also a pearly border, grown very slowly over four years. Which features help distinguish it from a melanocytic lesion?","choices":["The absence of any surface change and the patient's age","The presence of ulceration and rapid growth","The pearly border and the slow growth","The stippled pigmentation and the facial site"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pigmented basal cell carcinoma can mimic melanocytic disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 44-year-old shows you a mole on her upper back. One half does not match the other, the edge is notched, and there are brown, black and pink areas within it. It is 8 mm across and she says it has changed this year. Which recognition criteria does this lesion meet?","choices":["Border irregularity and evolution only, since colour variation within a lesion is a benign feature","Diameter and evolution only, since asymmetry and border irregularity are not part of the mnemonic","Asymmetry, border irregularity, colour variegation, diameter over 6 mm, and evolution — all five","Asymmetry, border irregularity and colour variegation only, since 8 mm is below the diameter threshold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — every element of the mnemonic is present. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 62-year-old has a slowly enlarging papule beside the nose with a central erosion. When you stretch the skin, fine vessels become obvious across a pearly, translucent surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Amelanotic melanoma","Nodular basal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — stretching accentuates the telangiectasias. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 60-year-old is being examined for a suspicious lesion. Which additional sites does the deck ask you to include, and when is the oral cavity specifically indicated?","choices":["Palms, soles and nails in all; the oral mucosa specifically when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease","Palms, soles and nails in every patient; the oral mucosa specifically when melanoma is possible; and the regional nodes for every lesion whatever its risk","The affected area alone, since the total-body survey, the acral sites and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist","The scalp and trunk only, since acral sites and the oral mucosa are rarely involved in cutaneous malignancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the oral examination is tied to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 64-year-old has a biopsy-confirmed superficial basal cell carcinoma on the upper back, with no high-risk features. Which topical regimen fits the deck, and what must follow it?","choices":["Imiquimod five nights weekly for 6 to 10 weeks, or fluorouracil twice daily for up to 12 weeks; clinical clearance must be confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod twice daily for 6 to 10 weeks, or fluorouracil five nights weekly for up to 12 weeks; clearance can be assumed once inflammation settles","Vismodegib orally for 6 to 10 weeks; clinical clearance must be confirmed afterwards","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy every 2 to 3 weeks for 12 weeks; clearance must be confirmed afterwards"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — confirming clearance is part of the regimen, not optional. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A patient with known Kaposi sarcoma has marked lower limb oedema but only three visible skin lesions. How should the oedema be interpreted?","choices":["Marked oedema indicates the classic rather than the epidemic form, and its severity gauges the disease burden","Marked oedema can occur even with few or no visible lesions, so its severity must not be used to gauge disease burden","Marked oedema indicates a correspondingly low disease burden, consistent with the small number of visible skin lesions","Marked oedema is not a feature of Kaposi sarcoma at any stage and points instead to venous thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the skin can look almost clear while disease is extensive. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 52-year-old's melanoma is reported at 0.6 mm Breslow thickness with no adverse features. Which re-excision margin applies?","choices":["A 1 to 2 cm margin","A 3 cm margin","A 1 cm margin","A 0.5 to 1 cm margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lesions under 1 mm take a 1 cm margin. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 67-year-old has had a warty plaque beside one fingernail for two years. It has been treated repeatedly as a wart and once as a fungal infection, and now oozes and bleeds with nail plate destruction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nail unit melanoma, which the deck calls the most common malignant nail tumour","Nail unit basal cell carcinoma, which the deck calls the most common malignant nail tumour","Onychomatricoma, which the deck calls the most common malignant nail tumour","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma or Bowen disease, the most common malignant nail tumour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a lesion repeatedly labelled wart, paronychia or fungus is the classic history. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 71-year-old retired roofer has several rough patches on his bald scalp. Each is about 4 mm, pink, and you notice them more when you run a gloved finger over the scalp than when you look. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Superficial basal cell carcinoma","Solar lentigo","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the sandpaper texture is often felt before it is seen. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A patient asks how likely his actinically induced squamous cell carcinoma is to spread. Which figure does the deck give, and what raises it?","choices":["An estimated 30 to 70%, raised further by high-risk sites or features, immunosuppression, multiple tumours, recurrence, or nodal involvement","An estimated 3 to 7%, and nothing raises it further, since metastatic risk is fixed at the moment of diagnosis whatever the site or immune status","Under 0.1%, raised only by nodal involvement already present at the time of diagnosis","An estimated 3 to 7%, raised substantially by high-risk sites or features, immunosuppression, multiple tumours, recurrence, or nodal involvement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the baseline is modest but modifiable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man's biopsy reports squamous cell carcinoma in situ on the forearm with no high-risk features. Which options does the deck give?","choices":["Hedgehog pathway inhibitors, vismodegib or sonidegib","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery only","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — three options for appropriately selected in situ disease. Or curettage and electrodesiccation. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a rapidly enlarging pink nodule on the shoulder with no pigment at all. It has been present six weeks. Which melanoma subtype should you consider, and why is it dangerous?","choices":["Nodular melanoma — it grows rapidly, is often amelanotic, and may lack the classic recognition features","Superficial spreading melanoma — it grows rapidly, is often amelanotic, and may lack the classic features","Lentigo maligna melanoma — it grows rapidly on chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults","Acral lentiginous melanoma — it grows rapidly and is often amelanotic on the palms and soles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the absence of pigment and of the mnemonic features delays diagnosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"You suspect a basal cell carcinoma on a patient's shoulder. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic step, and why does it matter which subtype comes back?","choices":["Sentinel lymph node biopsy, because nodal status determines treatment selection","Shave or punch biopsy, because the HISTOLOGIC subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Shave or punch biopsy, because the CLINICAL subtype determines behaviour and dictates treatment selection","Wide local excision with 1 cm margins, because tissue diagnosis and treatment are achieved together"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — histology, not clinical appearance, drives the plan. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A patient has a single nail with longitudinal erythronychia, distal subungual hyperkeratosis and splinter haemorrhages. Which benign tumours does the deck associate with this pattern?","choices":["Digital myxoid cyst or subungual exostosis","Melanocytic naevus or lentigo","Onychopapilloma or onychomatricoma","Glomus tumour or pyogenic granuloma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — single-nail longitudinal change with distal hyperkeratosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 68-year-old has a single well-demarcated actinic keratosis on the dorsal hand. Which is the most appropriate treatment, and what should you tell her to expect?","choices":["Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy; the lesion will blister and resolve completely within 24 hours","Topical fluorouracil to the whole forearm; the field will inflame and settle over 10 to 14 days","Surgical excision with 5 mm margins; the wound will heal over 10 to 14 days","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy; the lesion will crust and disappear over 10 to 14 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lesion-directed therapy suits an isolated lesion with clear borders. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 61-year-old's melanoma measures 0.9 mm Breslow thickness, but her report notes ulceration and a high mitotic rate. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Sentinel lymph node biopsy is not indicated, because the threshold is a fixed 1.0 mm regardless of other features","Sentinel lymph node biopsy is mandatory, because ulceration alone converts this to advanced disease","Sentinel lymph node biopsy should be deferred until the lesion recurs locally after re-excision","Sentinel lymph node biopsy should be offered or discussed, because additional histologic risk factors lower the threshold to 0.8 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ulceration, high mitotic rate and lymphovascular invasion are the three named factors. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
